# /etc/waxseal/anchor.env — for waxseal-anchor@.service
#
#   install -o root -g waxseal -m 0640 anchor.env.example /etc/waxseal/anchor.env
#
# NO REAL VALUES IN THIS FILE. It is an example in a public repository.
#
# Loaded with a LEADING DASH in the unit, so a missing file is not a failure.

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The witness credential.
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WAXSEAL_WITNESS_API_KEY and never WAXSEAL_API_KEY. REMOTE.md section 8: a
# witness holding the chain's write credential could append forged entries to
# the very chain it exists to cross-check. The CLI reads only this variable
# for witness deposits, so setting the wrong one is a silent no-op rather than
# a leak — but set the right one.
#
# Do NOT put the chain server's WAXSEAL_API_KEY in this file. This unit runs as
# the chain's own user; it does not need one, and a write credential in a file
# read by the anchor path is a credential in one more place.
#WAXSEAL_WITNESS_API_KEY=

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The sinks. At least one, and each under an authority that is NOT yours.
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Word-split by systemd. This is the security argument of the whole anchor
# step: a published root the trail's writer can rewrite proves nothing
# (docs/architecture/deployment.md section 5). The CLIENT running here, beside
# the chain, is the intended topology; the SINKS below must not be.
#
#   --tsa-url URL                 an RFC 3161 timestamp authority.
#   --ots-calendar URL            an OpenTimestamps calendar.
#   --witness URL                 another party's waxseal witness endpoint.
#   --evm-rpc URL (x2 or more)    plus --evm-liveness ADDR: a fourth
#                                 independently-recording anchor domain.
#                                 Two or more endpoints, because one endpoint
#                                 cannot disagree with itself and a
#                                 "cross-check" against a single voice is not
#                                 one. Ledger WRITES additionally need
#                                 WAXSEAL_EVM_SIGNER_CMD — an external signer
#                                 program, never a private key on argv or in a
#                                 flag.
#
# Leaving this empty makes `waxseal anchor` write a local .anchors sidecar and
# publish nothing: an anchor in name only.
#
#WAXSEAL_ANCHOR_SINK_ARGS=--tsa-url https://tsa.example.invalid/tsr --ots-calendar https://calendar.example.invalid

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The ledger signer, only if --evm-* is used above.
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# An executable this CLI invokes with three verbs — address / sign-digest /
# sign-tx. The key material stays behind that boundary and never crosses into
# waxseal's process, the same boundary WAXSEAL_API_KEY and
# WAXSEAL_WITNESS_API_KEY already draw.
#WAXSEAL_EVM_SIGNER_CMD=/usr/local/libexec/waxseal-evm-signer
